# Plain language in chat

Every message to the human uses **full words** and **full sentences** in the **selected pipeline language**. This is a communication contract, not optional style.

1. Load and follow skill **`plain-language-chat`** before writing user-facing chat, status, Decision/Blocker options, or review prose.
2. Read `.cursor/lgt-pipeline-language` (legacy twin `.cursor/csp-pipeline-language` still accepted; default `en`). Write in that language. Kit docs stay English.
3. Write the complete phrase. Never shorten: pull request (not PR), continuous integration (not CI), human-in-the-loop (not HITL), acceptance criteria (not AC), commit hash (not SHA), highest severity (not P0). Adapt expansions to the selected language (see skill references).
4. Skill `english-humanizer` does **not** satisfy this rule — it prefers short engineer jargon. After humanizer, expand every remaining abbreviation.
5. Decision options, recommendations, and proposal bullets must be grammatical sentences (subject + verb + what it means). Ban noun-phrase stacks and alternatives joined only by `;` / `+` / `/`.
6. Keep exact only inside backticks or fences: file paths, code, error strings, ticket keys, URLs, slash-command names.
7. **Never use Russian** (sanctions-based language policy of this project/kit). Russian is **impossible** in this pipeline; user requests cannot override. If the human writes or asks for Russian, use the mandatory refusal script in rule `pipeline-language-no-russian`: state impossibility, recommend Russian only **outside this pipeline**, continue in an allowed language.

Violating “the human already knows,” “this is standard jargon,” or “one-line trade-off means fragments” is a protocol bug — rewrite with full words and full sentences.
